 \\pmod{5}$. $450 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5}$. So $2^{120} + 450 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}$. No.\n\nIf $n = 6$: $\\lfloor\\sqrt{6}\\rfloor = 2$, so $(2!)^{6!} + 450 = 2^{720} + 450$. Does $6 \\mid 2^{720} + 450$? Need divisible by 2 and 3. $2^{720} + 450$ is even (both even). For mod 3: $2^{720} \\equiv (-1)^{720} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}$. $450 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{3}$. So $2^{720} + 450 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3}$. No.\n\nIf $n = 7$: $\\lfloor\\sqrt{7}\\rfloor = 2$, so $(2!)^{7!} + 450 = 2^{5040} + 450$. Does $7 \\mid 2^{5040} + 450$? By Fermat's little theorem, $2^6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}$. $5040 = 6 \\times 840$, so $2^{5040} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}$. $450 = 64 \\times 7 + 2$, so $450 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}$. So $2^{5040} + 450 \\equiv 1 + 2 = 3 \\pmod{7}$. No.\n\nIf $n = 8$: $\\lfloor\\sqrt{8}\\rfloor = 2$, so $(2!)^{8!} + 450 = 2^{40320} + 450$. Does $8 \\mid 2^{40320} + 450$? $2^{40320} \\equiv 0 \\pmod{8}$ (since $40320 \\geq 3$). $450 = 56 \\times 8 + 2$, so $450 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{8}$. So $2^{40320} + 450 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{8}$. No.\n\nIf $n = 9$: $\\lfloor\\sqrt{9}\\rfloor = 3$, so $(3!)^{9!} + 450 = 6^{362880} + 450$. Does $9 \\mid 6^{362880} + 450$? $6^{362880} \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9}$ (since $6^2 = 36 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9}$, and $362880 \\geq 2$). $450 = 50 \\times 9$, so $450 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9}$. So $6^{362880} + 450 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9}$. Yes!\n\nSo $n = 9$ works.\n\nIf $n = 10$: $\\lfloor\\sqrt{10}\\rfloor = 3$, so $(3!)^{10!} + 450 = 6^{3628800} + 450$. Does $10 \\mid 6^{3628800} + 450$? Need divisible by 2 and 5. It's even (both even). For mod 5: $6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}$, so $6^{3628800} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}$. $450 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5}$. So $6^{3628800} + 450 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}$. No.\n\nIf $n = 11$: $\\lfloor\\sqrt{11}\\rfloor = 3$, so $(3!)^{11!} + 450 = 6^{39916800} + 450$. Does $11 \\mid 6^{39916800} + 450$? By FLT, $6^{10} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}$. $39916800 = 10 \\times 3991680$, so $6^{39916800} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}$. $450 = 40 \\times 11 + 10$, so $450 \\equiv 10 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{11}$. So $6^